            <ArticleTitle>Ulnar-sided wrist pain. Part I: anatomy and physical examination.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>733-45</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00256-009-0775-x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Ulnar-sided wrist pain is a common complaint, and it presents a diagnostic challenge for hand surgeons and radiologists. The complex anatomy of this region, combined with the small size of structures and subtle imaging findings, compound this problem. A thorough understanding of ulnar-sided wrist anatomy and a systematic clinical examination of this region are essential in arriving at an accurate diagnosis. In part I of this review, ulnar-sided wrist anatomy and clinical examination are discussed for a more comprehensive understanding of ulnar-sided wrist pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
